Chest X‑ray shows focal lobar or multifocal patchy airspace opacities, often dense consolidation with air bronchograms, commonly unilateral or bilateral, occasionally with blurring of cardiac/diaphragmatic borders (silhouette sign) and small pleural effusion.
On chest X-ray, lung consolidation appears as focal or patchy air-space opacity, often lobar or multifocal, with air bronchograms, possible unilateral or bilateral distribution, perihilar or basilar predominance, and occasional blunting of the costophrenic angle.
Chest X‑ray commonly shows focal or lobar air‑space opacities appearing as dense, fluffy consolidations with air bronchograms; variations include patchy multifocal opacities, bilateral interstitial or alveolar patterns, and occasional small pleural effusions.
On chest X-ray, pneumonia typically appears as focal lobar consolidation or patchy air space opacities with increased radiodensity, often showing air bronchograms, unilateral or bilateral distribution, possible multilobar involvement and occasional small pleural effusions.
Patchy or lobar air-space opacities on chest radiographs, often unilateral but sometimes bilateral, with fluffy consolidation margins, air bronchograms, silhouetting of adjacent cardiac or diaphragmatic borders, and occasional blunted costophrenic angles from effusion.
On chest X-ray, pneumonia appears as focal or patchy air-space consolidation with increased opacity in lung fields, often lobar or multifocal, sometimes showing air bronchograms, with variable size, density, and location between patients.
Patchy or confluent air-space opacities on chest radiograph, often lobar or segmental, appearing as dense, ill-defined consolidations; may show air bronchograms, unilateral or bilateral distribution, occasionally small pleural effusion or interstitial reticular markings.
Patchy to confluent airspace opacities appear in one or more lobes, sometimes forming lobar consolidation with air bronchograms, blurring cardiac or diaphragmatic borders, and varying from focal unilateral to diffuse bilateral involvement.
On chest X-ray, pneumonia commonly appears as focal lobar or multifocal patchy airspace opacities with air bronchograms, ranging from dense homogeneous consolidation to scattered peribronchial or interstitial infiltrates; may be unilateral or bilateral and sometimes with small pleural effusions.
Patchy or lobar air space opacities on chest radiograph, often unilateral or bilateral, with air bronchograms, silhouette sign, and possible pleural effusion; distribution varies from focal perihilar consolidation to multifocal diffuse peripheral involvement.
